\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A word with two triangle orientations}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Choose orthonormal coordinates in #{E=\mathbb C^3} and take
two coupling endomorphisms
##{Y_1=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&2&0\\0&0&3\end{pmatrix},
\qquad Y_2=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\1&1&0\\1&\ui&1\end{pmatrix}.}
Their [ordered Gram pair](fdip-018P) has
#{A=\operatorname{diag}(1,4,9)} and
##{B=\begin{pmatrix}
1&1&1\\1&2&1-\ui\\1&1+\ui&3
\end{pmatrix}.}
Writing #{a_i} for the diagonal entries of #{A}, the trace
#{\trace(ABA^2B^2)} is
##{\sum_{i,j,k=1}^3 a_i a_j^2 B_{ij}B_{jk}B_{ki}.}
For repeated indices the product has only diagonal entries
or a diagonal entry times a modulus square. Collecting all
these real terms gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\sum_i a_i^3 B_{ii}^3\\
&\quad+\sum_{i\ne j}|B_{ij}|^2
 \bigl(a_i^3 B_{ii}+a_i a_j^2(B_{ii}+B_{jj})\bigr)=32243.
\end{aligned}}}

\p{With distinct indices the cycle #{1,2,3} has product
#{1-\ui}. Summing its three choices of starting point gives
weight #{1\cdot4^2+4\cdot9^2+9\cdot1^2=349}.
The reverse cycle has product #{1+\ui} and weight
#{1\cdot9^2+9\cdot4^2+4\cdot1^2=229}. Hence
##{\trace(ABA^2B^2)=32243+349(1-\ui)+229(1+\ui)
=32821-120\ui.}
By \ref{fdip-018Y} no antiunitary preserves this pair.
Its commutator has determinant #{-240\ui} by \ref{fdip-0193}.}
